{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '165,218p' paper.tex; tail -n 16 memory.md; if [ -f conclusions.md ]; then cat conclusions.md; fi; ls .codex/skills/memory-log/scripts; python .codex/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py --help; sha256sum paper.tex references.bib references_provenance.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '165,218p' paper.tex; tail -n 16 memory.md; if [ -f conclusions.md ]; then cat conclusions.md; fi; ls .codex/skills/memory-log/scripts; python .codex/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py --help; sha256sum paper.tex references.bib references_provenance.json\"","aggregated_output":"Global ILR & 0.82960 & 0.05737 & 0.06878 & 0.49235\\\\\nRaw $L_\\infty$ & 0.84665 & 0.03490 & 0.04807 & 0.48896\\\\\nBonferroni & 0.84886 & 0.02548 & 0.03890 & 0.50799\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json#/aggregate]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\\end{table}\n\nThe local procedure has lower aggregate errors and reference mass. Pooling shifted and exchangeable conditions, however, cannot establish universal superiority or test exchangeable-data validity; condition-specific results are essential.\n\n\\subsection{Homoscedastic and Heteroscedastic Conditions}\nFigure~\\ref{fig:coverage} shows that the homoscedastic conditions provide a meaningful negative control. Mean local and global utilities are 0.85319 versus 0.85326 for H3, and 0.87887 versus 0.88028 for H10. Their mean reference masses are nearly equal: 0.55883 versus 0.55803 and 0.45106 versus 0.45105. Thus there is no consistent homoscedastic advantage to offset the complexity of learning a scale. We do not interpret these small observed differences as a precisely established disadvantage; no condition-specific inferential interval is supplied.\n% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json#/cases/H3]]\n% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json#/cases/H10]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/coverage_by_condition.pdf}\n\\caption{Joint coverage by condition, dimension, and nominal level. Small dots show individual seeds; horizontal colored marks show means; dashed lines show nominal coverage. H: homoscedastic; B: boundary-concentrated heteroscedastic; S: B response mechanism with shifted test covariates. Panels share an axis spanning all observations. Shifted coverage is an empirical stress test, not a validity guarantee.}\n\\label{fig:coverage}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\\end{figure}\n\nIn B3 and B10, local utility rises to 0.89045 and 0.90820, compared with global utilities 0.84703 and 0.84162. The contrast is not merely a pooled score effect: fixed-group error decreases from 0.07770 to 0.01816 in B3 and from 0.07181 to 0.01723 in B10. Reference mass also decreases, from 0.47580 to 0.39349 and from 0.49497 to 0.32211. Local mean coverage at nominal 0.90 is 0.89689 in B3 and 0.90706 in B10. These joint observations support improved allocation under the evaluated heteroscedastic mechanism, while the remaining discrepancies preclude claiming exact conditional calibration.\n% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json#/cases/B3]]\n% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json#/cases/B10]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\nRaw-coordinate methods are useful alternatives rather than uniformly weak baselines. Bonferroni is conservative in these heteroscedastic conditions: at nominal 0.90 its mean coverage is 0.93465 in B3 and 0.93717 in B10. Its group errors are lower than global ILR's but higher than the local procedure's. Raw $L_\\infty$ also improves some global-ILR summaries without matching the local procedure's aggregate tradeoff. The comparison concerns complete region constructions. It cannot separate coordinate geometry, calibration multiplicity, and learned adaptation into isolated causal contributions.\n% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json#/cases/B3]]\n% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json#/cases/B10]]\n\n\\subsection{Shift Stress Test and the Cost of Coverage Repair}\nThe shift exposes a substantial gap between global calibration and local scaling in the recorded data. At nominal 0.90, global ILR covers 0.74748 of responses in S3 and 0.70190 in S10; local scaling covers 0.89933 and 0.91610. At nominal 0.95 the corresponding global means are 0.85067 and 0.81405, versus local means 0.94979 and 0.95768. The raw-coordinate baselines mitigate some of this undercoverage but do not reach the same mean coverage in these conditions. This is evidence about the specified covariate shift, not evidence that ordinary split conformal becomes valid under arbitrary shift.\n% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json#/cases/S3]]\n% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json#/cases/S10]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/reference_mass_by_condition.pdf}\n\\caption{Reference mass, averaged over the two nominal levels within each seed. Dots show seeds and marks show means under the shared mixed reference measure. The S3 local region has greater mass than global ILR; improved coverage must not be summarized as uniformly smaller regions. These values are not intrinsic simplex volumes.}\n\\label{fig:mass}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\\end{figure}\n\nFigure~\\ref{fig:mass} makes the efficiency qualification explicit. S3 local mass is 0.50795, exceeding global ILR's 0.47945. A global region that misses many shifted responses can look artificially economical. Local scaling pays additional mass while restoring empirical coverage in that condition. S10 instead has local mass 0.47159 versus global mass 0.49482. The two outcomes rule out a uniform region-size claim even within this limited evaluation. They also illustrate why mass and coverage should be inspected together rather than collapsing the interpretation into one scalar.\n% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json#/cases/S3]]\n% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json#/cases/S10]]\n\n\\subsection{Alignment Controls and Attribution Limits}\nThe constant-covariate control agrees with global ILR, with aggregate utility 0.82960. Permuting training residual targets gives utility 0.82985, compared with 0.87723 for aligned local scaling; the paired interval for the aligned-minus-permuted utility difference is [0.04313, 0.05109]. This comparison supports the relevance of covariate--residual alignment to the complete procedure under this design. Permutation also changes model selection and estimation, so it does not isolate a particular functional component. All declared control checks pass in the final record, which is a diagnostic result rather than a separate scientific guarantee.\n\n### 2026-11-12T23:25:30.811914+00:00 | step=paper | scope=paper\n- summary: Verified final qualification and selected condition-dependent local-scale thesis; emitting evidence bundle and inspecting source.\n- evidence: final.qualification completed/qualified/true; six final seed records; no downstream_study or task_names. Inspected selected source and evaluator; no component selection diagnostics or matched final original-scale comparator. Existing 25-reference retrieval includes irrelevant results and will be replaced with directly consulted targeted foundational sources. No prohibited literature accessed.\n- next_action: Build provenance-backed final-result figures, consult safe literature directly, draft anonymous ICLR manuscript and validate.\n\n### 2026-11-12T23:39:06.497670+00:00 | step=draft | scope=paper\n- summary: Drafted anonymous condition-first manuscript, two quantitative composite figures, supplementary tables, 42 claim mappings and reproduction instructions.\n- evidence: All measurements derive from final.evidence.episodes; final mean utility 0.8772268474776957 and paired global interval [0.043901240346666696,0.050616967867983816]. Explicitly retained negative H conditions, S3 mass increase, shifted-validity and component-attribution limits. Replaced irrelevant retrieval with 25 directly identified foundational references and stored consulted metadata/abstracts. Initial validation repaired external-symlink evidence links via local source copies, SI unchecked inputs via embedded tables, and LLM disclosure to final-SI subsection. Native apply_patch missing; used private compatible apply_patch command. No evaluation reruns.\n- next_action: Finish page layout and rendered inspection, resolve bibliography author metadata, rerun grounding and evidence checks; document canonical figure-validator import and system-TeX symlink limitations.\n\n### 2026-11-12T23:46:21.646993+00:00 | step=final | scope=paper\n- summary: paper.tex and claims_grounding.json are consistent with grounded inputs; anonymous eight-page body and substantive supplement compile successfully.\n- evidence: Canonical section/citation checks pass with 25 references; 43 claim mappings and bibliography/provenance checks pass. Scientific-evidence verifier has zero dangling references. Strict compile workflow and rendered-SI check pass after private Poppler installation and a canonical-system-config TeX wrapper. Canonical figure entrypoint lacks its src package, so local canonical-schema, strategy density, resolution, exact-table, aggregate and rendered-language checks passed; parent figure validation remains authoritative. Local geometry gives final body text extent 0.977 of nine-inch height, not a character-count proxy. PDF has 13 pages including references and SI. Two quantitative composite figures export PDF and 300-DPI PNG; overview is correctly reserved for parent rendering, not falsely claimed complete. Portable original evaluator, learner, pinned dependency and frozen final resources are packaged. Exact copied evaluator digest matches final identifier; selected source-tree identity is not independently proved. Final qualification completed/qualified/true is preserved, as are negative H findings, S3 mass increase, unknown gate/failure causes, shift/conditional-validity limits and lack of component attribution. No scientific evaluator rerun or prohibited literature access occurred.\n- next_action: Parent should synchronously render conceptual_overview, recompile and run authoritative final venue/figure checks; no scientific claim should be strengthened without new evidence.\n\nappend_memory.py\nusage: append_memory.py [-h] [--write-conclusions] [--event-json EVENT_JSON]\n                        [--event-file EVENT_FILE]\n                        [--local-memory-md LOCAL_MEMORY_MD]\n                        [--global-memory-md GLOBAL_MEMORY_MD]\n                        [--global-memory-jsonl GLOBAL_MEMORY_JSONL]\n                        [--conclusions-md CONCLUSIONS_MD]\n                        [--conclusions-history-jsonl CONCLUSIONS_HISTORY_JSONL]\n                        [--conclusions-text CONCLUSIONS_TEXT]\n                        [--cycle-index CYCLE_INDEX]\n                        [--worker-index WORKER_INDEX]\n                        [--gate-trigger GATE_TRIGGER]\n                        [--gate-reason GATE_REASON]\n\nAppend one memory event, or atomically rewrite conclusions.md.\n\noptions:\n  -h, --help            show this help message and exit\n  --write-conclusions   Switch into conclusions-write mode (validates and\n                        replaces conclusions.md).\n  --event-json EVENT_JSON\n                        Event payload as JSON object string\n  --event-file EVENT_FILE\n                        Path to event JSON file\n  --local-memory-md LOCAL_MEMORY_MD\n                        Local workspace memory markdown path\n  --global-memory-md GLOBAL_MEMORY_MD\n                        Global markdown memory path (event mode)\n  --global-memory-jsonl GLOBAL_MEMORY_JSONL\n                        Global JSONL memory path (event mode)\n  --conclusions-md CONCLUSIONS_MD\n                        Path of conclusions.md to overwrite (--write-\n                        conclusions mode)\n  --conclusions-history-jsonl CONCLUSIONS_HISTORY_JSONL\n                        Path of conclusions_history.jsonl to append a record\n                        to\n  --conclusions-text CONCLUSIONS_TEXT\n                        Conclusion paragraph (omit to read from stdin)\n  --cycle-index CYCLE_INDEX\n                        Current cycle index for the history record\n  --worker-index WORKER_INDEX\n                        Current worker index for the history record\n  --gate-trigger GATE_TRIGGER\n                        Gate trigger letter (a|b|c|d) recorded in history\n  --gate-reason GATE_REASON\n                        One-sentence reason for the update; for trigger d name\n                        the dimension\n0305be1f19abaa4202b52a65850c34b914db9d23611a8c61827c91a6c08ce157  paper.tex\nf4f0f663d2903d0a0ebedbe9a9d302bfac9ccaaf03f542a72f3e150aa721c55e  references.bib\n4297167404172377a73ed72ca7dc14a3f914885815adc109a62eb8bd19f86092  references_provenance.json\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
